      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is characterized by transient left ventricular dysfunction in patients with normal findings on coronary angiography. The simultaneous incidence of coronary vessel disease and takotsubo cardiomyopathy is described."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE REPORT",
          "content": "A 72-year-old, previously healthy female patient reported about chest pain. Acute emotional and physical stress were denied. Cardiac enzymes and electrocardiogram presented typical findings of myocardial infarction. Coronary angiography revealed a coronary one-vessel disease with occlusion of the second branch of the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery which did not explain the severe reduction of left ventricular function. After 4 months, left ventricular function and electrocardiogram had returned to normal."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Coronary artery disease does not rule out takotsubo cardiomyopathy."
        }
      ]},
